<p>EPUB is the smarter choice for most ereaders because it re-flows to fit your screen, lets you change font size and spacing, and stays readable on every modern device. If you only find a PDF edition you prefer, converting it to EPUB is straightforward with a tool like <a href="/blog/pdf-to-epub-free-conversion-2026/">this free PDF to EPUB converter</a>.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-download">How to Download NASA E-Books as EPUB</h2>

<p>Getting a NASA e-book onto a reader takes about two minutes. The whole flow works in any browser, needs no account, and costs nothing.</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Open the NASA e-Books page.</strong> Go to <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/ebooks/" rel="noopener">nasa.gov/ebooks</a> and pick a category (Aeronautics, NASA History, or Science) or open a Featured title.</li>
  <li><strong>Open a book.</strong> Click &ldquo;Overview&rdquo; on any title to land on its official e-book page with the download buttons.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose EPUB.</strong> Click the EPUB download link. The file saves to your computer &mdash; no DRM, no Amazon account, no store checkout.</li>
  <li><strong>Move it to your reader.</strong> On a Kobo or PocketBook, connect over USB and drop the file into the &ldquo;Books&rdquo; folder. On a Kindle, use Send to Kindle. On a phone or tablet, open the file in your favorite EPUB app.</li>
</ol>

<p>Because the EPUB files are DRM-free, you are not locked to one device family. The same file opens on a Kobo, a PocketBook, a Boox, a phone, a tablet, or a computer &mdash; exactly the portability that DRM-free library building provides (see our <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB library guide</a>).</p>

<h2 id="kindle-transfer">Reading NASA E-Books on a Kindle</h2>

<p>Kindle does not open EPUB files natively, but Amazon&rsquo;s <strong>Send to Kindle</strong> service converts an EPUB for you and delivers it wirelessly. The EPUB format is the recommended input for Send to Kindle, so NASA&rsquo;s EPUB files are the best fit here.</p>

<ol>
  <li>Go to <a href="https://www.amazon.com/sendtokindle" rel="noopener">amazon.com/sendtokindle</a> or use the Send to Kindle app.</li>
  <li>Drag the NASA EPUB file into the upload window.</li>
  <li>Send it to your Kindle (or the Kindle app) &mdash; Amazon converts it automatically.</li>
  <li>Wait a minute for the file to appear in your library, then open and read.</li>
</ol>

<p>If you would rather do the conversion yourself with full control over the result, our <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">Calibre to Kindle workflow</a> walks through the whole pipeline, and the <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre library management guide</a> shows how to keep thousands of titles organized once your collection grows.</p>

<h2 id="reading-options">Kobo, PocketBook, Tablets, and Beyond</h2>

<p>NASA e-books are a great fit for open-format readers because no DRM means no restrictions. On a Kobo or PocketBook you simply connect the device, copy the EPUB into the books folder, and eject &mdash; the device picks it up immediately. On Android or iOS, share the file to your reader app of choice, or save it to your files app and open it from there. For anyone comparing which device handles DRM-free EPUB best, the <a href="/blog/best-ereader-2026-expert-roundup/">2026 e-reader roundup</a> is a useful starting point.</p>

<p>One caveat applies to image-heavy NASA books: science and Hubble titles are illustrated, and on a small monochrome e-ink screen the photos look better on a reader with a larger, higher-resolution panel or on a tablet. The EPUB version still reads fine everywhere; it is only the full-color photography that benefits from a bigger screen.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">The Bottom Line</h2>

<p>NASA&rsquo;s free e-book library is a genuinely excellent, 100% legitimate source of DRM-free EPUB downloads. It covers aeronautics, space history, Hubble, and the space station; every title offers a direct EPUB and PDF download; and the files move freely between Kindle, Kobo, PocketBook, tablet, and phone. Since it costs nothing and requires no account, it is one of the best ways to start (or grow) a personal EPUB library without spending a cent.</p>

<p>Just download the EPUB, send it to your reader, and enjoy reading NASA&rsquo;s own writing about the universe &mdash; free, and yours to keep.</p>

